'''''Juncus pallidus''''', commonly known as the '''great soft-rush'''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17 }}</ref> '''pale rush''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=JUPA9|taxon=Juncus pallidus|accessdate=22 January 2016}}</ref> '''giant rush,''' or '''leafless rush''' is a species of [[Juncus|rush]] that is native to southern [[Australia]], [[New Zealand]], [[Norfolk Island]], and [[Lord Howe Island]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nzpcn.org.nz/flora_details.aspx?ID=2173|title=Juncus pallidus {{!}} New Zealand Plant Conservation Network|website=www.nzpcn.org.nz|access-date=2018-05-07}}</ref>  It is a vigorous, tufted, [[tussock (grass)|tussock]]-forming, [[rhizome|rhizomatous]] [[perennial plant|perennial]] [[herbaceous plant|herb]] with [[culm (botany)|culms]] growing to 70–135&nbsp;cm in height.  The inflorescence, which is 25–185&nbsp;mm long, contains many straw coloured flowers, each with six floral segments.  It is usually found in moist, nutrient-poor soils subject to periodic flooding, such as fresh and brackish waterways, including swamps, creek banks, lake edges and sand [[Seep (hydrology)|seeps]].<ref>{{cite web |url= http://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&lvl=sp&name=Juncus~pallidus|title=Juncus pallidus|access-date= 2012-06-25 |author1=Wilson, K.L. |author2=Johnson, L.A.S. |author3=Bankoff, P. |work= PlantNET - New South Wales Flora Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens & Domain Trust, Sydney Australia}}</ref><ref name=ipbp>{{cite book |title=Inland Plants of the Bellarine Peninsula |author1=Longmore, Sue |author2=Smithyman, Steve |author3=Crawley, Matt |year=2010 |publisher= Bellarine Catchment Network }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.water.wa.gov.au/PublicationStore/first/84826.pdf |title=''Juncus pallidus'' Pale Rush |access-date=2012-06-25 |work=Juncaceae |publisher=Avon Catchment Council, Western Australia |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120428094755/http://www.water.wa.gov.au/PublicationStore/first/84826.pdf |archive-date=2012-04-28 }}</ref>

